    <div class="translation-source" id="trans1Source">
      <p>High-quality development is not about pursuing endless expansion at any cost, but about achieving growth that is balanced, coordinated, and sustainable. For a long time, many economies measured progress solely by GDP growth, ignoring issues such as income inequality, environmental degradation, and regional disparities. This narrow view of development is now being replaced by a more comprehensive approach that takes into account both economic efficiency and social equity.</p>
      <p>The Chinese path to high-quality development recognizes that prosperity must be shared to be meaningful. It means advancing supply-side structural reform, fostering innovation-driven growth, and strengthening social safety nets so that the benefits of development can reach all segments of society. At the same time, it emphasizes the importance of regional balance, ensuring that inland areas and rural communities are not left behind in the race toward modernization.</p>
      <p>The new development philosophy of innovative, coordinated, green, open, and shared development provides a guiding framework for this transformation. These five dimensions are not isolated goals but interconnected principles that reinforce one another. When a country can combine technological dynamism with social fairness, environmental stewardship with economic efficiency, and domestic vitality with global engagement, it has truly embarked on the road to high-quality development.</p>
    </div>

