      <div class="reference-text" id="trans2Reference">
        <p>Population aging is an important issue facing China's future development. By the end of 2025, the number of people aged 60 and above in China had exceeded 280 million, accounting for nearly 20 percent of the total population. Proactively responding to population aging is not only related to the well-being of hundreds of millions of elderly people but also to the high-quality development of the economy and society.</p>
        <p>The silver economy is becoming a new growth point. Local authorities have actively developed silver industries such as eldercare services, healthcare, and cultural tourism, continuously meeting the diverse and multi-level needs of the elderly. Meanwhile, smart eldercare has developed rapidly, with new models such as smart bracelets, remote medical consultations, and online courses for senior universities emerging one after another, making life for the elderly more convenient and colorful.</p>
        <p>It is worth mentioning that the concept of active aging is taking deep root among the people. An increasing number of seniors are no longer satisfied with aging at home; instead, they choose to step out, participate in community activities, learn new skills, and engage in volunteer services. It can be said that the silver economy has not only injected new momentum into economic growth but also helped older people find new meaning in life and a greater sense of social belonging.</p>
      </div>

