    <div class="translation-source" id="trans1Source">
      <p>Cultural confidence is not about looking back at the past with nostalgia, but about building a bridge between ancient wisdom and modern life. For a long time, traditional Chinese culture was often presented as something static and distant, something to be admired in museums but disconnected from daily reality. This narrow understanding is now being challenged by a new generation of creators who are finding fresh and creative ways to bring tradition into the present.</p>
      <p>The revival of interest in traditional culture recognizes that the past is not a burden but a source of strength. It means reinterpreting classical philosophy for contemporary readers, integrating traditional aesthetics into modern design, and promoting cultural education in schools and communities so that young people can grow up with a deep appreciation for their heritage. At the same time, it emphasizes the importance of openness and exchange, ensuring that cultural confidence does not lead to isolation but to greater mutual understanding with the world.</p>
      <p>Traditional Chinese thought has much to offer the global community. Concepts such as "harmony but not uniformity," "the unity of knowledge and action," and "all people under heaven are one family" provide valuable perspectives for addressing today's global challenges. When cultural traditions are kept alive through creative reinterpretation, they do not just connect people to their history — they offer shared wisdom for building a better future for all.</p>
    </div>

