    <div class="translation-source" id="trans1Source">
      <p>Chinese-style modernization is not about copying Western models wholesale, but about finding a path that suits China's own conditions while learning from the experiences of others. For a long time, modernization was often equated with Westernization, and countries that deviated from the Western path were seen as backward or abnormal. This narrow understanding is now being challenged by China's success, which demonstrates that there are multiple pathways to modernization, each shaped by a nation's unique history, culture, and social conditions.</p>
      <p>The Chinese model of modernization recognizes that development must be people-centered and inclusive. It means pursuing economic growth while ensuring social equity, promoting material progress while advancing cultural-ethical progress, and pursuing industrialization while protecting the ecological environment. At the same time, it emphasizes the importance of peaceful development, ensuring that China's rise does not come at the expense of other countries but contributes to the common prosperity of all nations.</p>
      <p>The significance of Chinese-style modernization extends far beyond China's borders. It offers a new choice for developing countries that wish to modernize while preserving their independence and cultural identity. When every nation is free to choose its own path to development, modernization will not just benefit a privileged few — it will become a shared global endeavor that brings progress and prosperity to all of humanity.</p>
    </div>

